{
  "passed": false,
  "security_concerns": [],
  "logic_errors": [
    {
      "file": "native/nvn_cpu_packed.h",
      "line": "wol_packed4444_candidate -> nvn_cpu_image_upload_type",
      "description": "The new WOL_PACKED4444 eligibility gate is default-off in the C helper, but the shipped launcher script exports WOL_PACKED4444=1 by default. This changes host_upload_type for 0x39 textures from GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE to 0x8033 in production. The new native-precision upload path in nvn_texture_decode_upload was only exercised by the new test, while the caller context confirms the existing unpacked path was previously the production path. This is a concrete behavioral regression risk: a device-tested opt-in is being force-enabled without evidence in this diff, potentially changing texture decode semantics and GL upload behavior for all 4444 textures.",
      "file": "native/threading.h",
      "line": "wol_guest_cpu",
      "description": "wol_guest_cpu converts sched_getcpu() to logical CPU using wol_cpu_map_offset. With WOL_CPU_OFFSET=4 enabled by the launcher, any host CPU outside the 4..6 range causes stop() with error 24. This is a fail-closed diagnostic, but the launcher default makes the diagnostic active in production; if the process migrates or the affinity query changes, guest SVC GetCurrentProcessorNumber becomes fatal rather than preserving the logical ABI. The diff does not demonstrate that all host CPUs are pinned exclusively to 4..6 for every thread, only the main thread and affinity mask construction are shown."
    },
    {
      "file": "native/nvn_render.h",
      "line": "wol_color_write_account",
      "description": "wol_color_write_account uses t->draws as a saturation counter, but the test only proves the counter increment behavior. The production code calls it unconditionally on nvn_render.target for every draw. If nvn_render.target is a texture with draws initialized to zero and a successful draw occurs, this changes the meaning of t->draws from a diagnostic counter to a proof proxy used by nvn_video_should_sample. The actual sampling behavior is in nvn_video_cadence.h: nvn_video_should_sample currently ignores t->draws except for proof and the new assertion in test_wol_color_write_proof.py checks that proof must remain. The diff does not show where proof is updated from draws in production, so the new counter may be dead code with no runtime effect, or it may unintentionally enable sparse sampling based on an unverified counter. This is a potential logic inconsistency."
    }
  ],
  "suggestions": [
    {
      "file": "native/nvn_cpu_packed.h",
      "suggestion": "Keep WOL_PACKED4444 default-off in the launcher until the native-precision upload path is proven by device measurement. The test only validates the C helper and exhaustive decode; it does not prove GL texture sampling or rendering equivalence on Mali-450."
    },
    {
      "file": "native/threading.h",
      "suggestion": "Document and enforce exclusive pinning of CPUs 4..6 when WOL_CPU_OFFSET=4; otherwise fall back to offset 0 instead of a fatal stop. The current fail-closed behavior may be acceptable for diagnostics but not as a production default."
    },
    {
      "file": "native/nvn_render.h",
      "suggestion": "Clarify the intended runtime impact of t->draws on nvn_video_should_sample. If it is only an accounting counter, avoid changing sampling policy without a full proof integration in the production path."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The diff introduces opt-in native precision 4444 uploads, host/logical CPU mapping, and color-write accounting. Tests mostly validate helper code in isolation, but the launcher defaults enable the new WOL_PACKED4444 and WOL_CPU_OFFSET paths in production without measured device evidence. The color-write accounting increment is safe in isolation, but its production effect on sampling is not shown and may be dead code or unintentionally alter sparse cadence."
}
